      "writtenOn": "2021-07-19T21:44:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Random idea... does this work?\n\n\n    #define FUNC_RIGHT(fn, nextExprFn)                                         \\\n    do {                                                                       \\\n        this-\u003enextToken();                                                     \\\n        if (!depth.increase()) {                                               \\\n            return skstd::nullopt;                                             \\\n        }                                                                      \\\n        skstd::optional\u003cDSLWrapper\u003cDSLExpression\u003e\u003e right \u003d this-\u003enextExprFn(); \\\n        if (!right) {                                                          \\\n            return skstd::nullopt;                                             \\\n        }                                                                      \\\n        result \u003d {{fn(std::move(**result), std::move(**right))}};              \\\n    } while (false)\n\n    #define OPERATOR_RIGHT(op, nextExprFn) FUNC_RIGHT(operator op, nextExprFn)\n\n\nAnd then later, you could use\n    \n    FUNC_RIGHT(LogicalXor, logicalAndExpression)\n\nIf it doesn\u0027t work, don\u0027t sweat it. But it seems like it ought to?\n\nFurther--it seems like it might even be possible to implement FUNC_RIGHT in terms of a template instead of a macro, but that can be an experiment for another day. The syntax of specifying the operator you want is probably finicky.",
      "range": {
