    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "19f00b32_79fb2cf4",
        "filename": "src/sksl/SkSLDSLParser.h",
        "patchSetId": 52
      },
      "lineNbr": 354,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-19T20:42:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We should assert that we aren\u0027t double-rewinding or double-accepting in accept() and rewind(), since that would break us (leaving the error reporter null forever). \n\n    SkASSERTF(fErrorReporter, \"Checkpoint was already accepted/rewound\");\n\n\nCuriosity: why do we do it this way instead of using `setErrorCount` to roll back speculative error reports? I\u0027m not sure why the old way wasn\u0027t sufficient.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 348,
        "startChar": 7,
        "endLine": 354,
        "endChar": 28
      },
      "revId": "7bc300ca72107799407460bbbe595b2d4357dc3a",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d50553b8_bde9cef6",
        "filename": "src/sksl/SkSLDSLParser.h",
        "patchSetId": 52
      },
      "lineNbr": 354,
      "author": {
        "id": 5640
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-20T19:23:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Done.\n\nAnd if we adopt the policy of \"never accept() in the face of errors\" (which I think is the right call, see below), then there\u0027s no reason to support setErrorCount in the first place. I intend to remove it altogether in a followup.",
      "parentUuid": "19f00b32_79fb2cf4",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 348,
        "startChar": 7,
        "endLine": 354,
        "endChar": 28
      },
      "revId": "7bc300ca72107799407460bbbe595b2d4357dc3a",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c29c7b3b_73bdb935",
        "filename": "src/sksl/SkSLDSLParser.h",
        "patchSetId": 52
      },
      "lineNbr": 362,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-19T20:42:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The Checkpoint might potentially be accepted even if an error occurred:\n\n    Checkpoint x; /* error occurs */; x.accept();\n\n\nIt seems as if this is just dropping the errors, on (I guess) the assumption that we would only accept a result with no errors. The original intent of the SkSLParser\u0027s Checkpoint class was that you could always accept or rewind the result, whether or not the result was an error. I\u0027m not sure if we actually use that flexibility today, but I\u0027d be disappointed to lose the option.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 360,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 362,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "7bc300ca72107799407460bbbe595b2d4357dc3a",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "90c5fdca_f7be58ae",
        "filename": "src/sksl/SkSLDSLParser.h",
        "patchSetId": 52
      },
      "lineNbr": 362,
      "author": {
        "id": 5640
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-07-20T19:23:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The whole point of a speculative parse is to check whether a particular construct can be parsed without error and only accept it if so. If you run into errors, that means you should be backing out and trying the other path. I can\u0027t think of a situation in which we\u0027d speculatively parse something, run into errors, and still want to accept it.\n\nI\u0027ve added an assert that there were no errors to the accept() call, because I agree that we should defend against that. And of course I remain open to having my mind changed, if you know of a situation in which this behavior would make sense.",
      "parentUuid": "c29c7b3b_73bdb935",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 360,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 362,
        "endChar": 9
      },
      "revId": "7bc300ca72107799407460bbbe595b2d4357dc3a",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
